---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import ProvenanceBlock from '../components/ProvenanceBlock.astro';
import { sectorMap, cpcProvenance, patentProvenance, formatCount } from '../lib/data';

const sectors = sectorMap ? Object.entries(sectorMap.sectors) : [];
const collisions = sectorMap ? Object.entries(sectorMap.cross_sector_prefix_collisions) : [];
---

<Base
  title="Business Development Targeting Engine — Jack Kelly"
  description="Ranking companies by near-term demand signals for IP legal services, using real USPTO patent data with a transparent, decomposable score."
>
  <p class="eyebrow">Project One</p>
  <h1>Business Development Targeting Engine</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    A law firm's IP practice needs to know which companies to call. Most firms rely on
    relationships, referrals, and whoever happens to read the news. This asks the
    question differently: which companies show the strongest signals of near-term
    demand for IP legal services, ranked, with the reasoning visible?
  </p>

  <h2>The framing</h2>

  <p>
    The premise is that patent activity is a usable proxy for legal services demand. A
    company filing heavily in a technology area is a company generating prosecution
    work, freedom-to-operate questions, licensing negotiations, and eventually
    litigation. That premise is defensible, and it is also wrong in specific ways that
    are set out below rather than buried.
  </p>

  <p>
    The approach mirrors panelist-acquisition work: stop contacting broadly, score
    prospects on data already available, and concentrate effort where it is most likely
    to convert.
  </p>

  <h2>Scoping the practice: CPC to sector</h2>

  <p>
    Before anything can be scored, the data has to be filtered to technologies the firm
    actually practices in. That means mapping the firm's industry teams onto the
    Cooperative Patent Classification scheme — and that mapping is analyst judgment, not
    a lookup. It is published here in full because the decisions are the interesting part.
  </p>

  {
    sectorMap ? (
      <>
        <div class="stats">
          <div class="stat">
            <span class="stat-value">{formatCount(sectorMap.cpc_symbols_in_scheme)}</span>
            <span class="stat-label">CPC symbols in scheme</span>
          </div>
          <div class="stat">
            <span class="stat-value">{sectorMap.subclasses_mapped}</span>
            <span class="stat-label">Subclasses mapped</span>
          </div>
          <div class="stat">
            <span class="stat-value">{sectorMap.subclass_coverage_pct}%</span>
            <span class="stat-label">of CPC subclass space</span>
          </div>
          <div class="stat">
            <span class="stat-value">{sectors.length}</span>
            <span class="stat-label">Practice sectors</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          Coverage is deliberately low. Four practice areas should not span all of human
          invention, and a mapping that claimed to would be describing nothing.
        </p>

        <h3>Cross-cutting decisions</h3>

        <p>
          These matter more than any individual code, because they determine what the
          resulting counts can and cannot be used for.
        </p>

        {sectorMap.decisions.map((d) => (
          <div class="callout">
            <span class="callout-label">{d.id}</span>
            <p>
              <strong>{d.decision}</strong>
            </p>
            <p>{d.rationale}</p>
            <p style="margin-bottom:0">
              <em>Consequence:</em> {d.consequence}
            </p>
          </div>
        ))}

        <h3>The mapping</h3>

        <p>
          Every code below was validated against CPC edition {sectorMap.cpc_edition}, and
          every title is attached from the published scheme rather than written by hand.
          The <em>rationale</em> column is judgment, and is the part worth disagreeing with.
        </p>

        {sectors.map(([key, sector]) => (
          <section>
            <h4 id={key}>{sector.label}</h4>
            <p>{sector.thesis}</p>
            <div class="table-scroll">
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="col">CPC</th>
                    <th scope="col">Official title</th>
                    <th scope="col">Rationale</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  {sector.codes.map((c) => (
                    <tr>
                      <td>
                        <code>{c.code}</code>
                      </td>
                      <td class="cpc-title">{c.official_title}</td>
                      <td>{c.why}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
            {sector.excluded_codes.length > 0 && (
              <div class="callout callout-warn">
                <span class="callout-label">Deliberately excluded</span>
                <ul>
                  {sector.excluded_codes.map((c) => (
                    <li>
                      <code>{c.code}</code> — {c.why}
                    </li>
                  ))}
                </ul>
              </div>
            )}
          </section>
        ))}

        <h3>Overlap between sectors</h3>
        {collisions.length > 0 ? (
          <>
            <p>
              These classifications are claimed by more than one sector, by design. It is
              why sector counts are reported independently and never summed.
            </p>
            <ul>
              {collisions.map(([prefix, owners]) => (
                <li>
                  <code>{prefix}</code> → {[...new Set(owners)].join(', ')}
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </>
        ) : (
          <p>No classification is currently claimed by two sectors.</p>
        )}

        <h3>What validation caught</h3>

        <div class="callout callout-warn">
          <span class="callout-label">A real defect, found by checking</span>
          <p>
            The first draft of this mapping used <code>H01L</code> for semiconductors and
            <code>H01L31/00</code> for photovoltaics. Both were retired in the 2023 CPC
            reorganisation into the <code>H10</code> series, and neither exists in edition{' '}
            {sectorMap.cpc_edition}.
          </p>
          <p style="margin-bottom:0">
            A retired CPC code does not raise an error. It silently matches zero patents.
            Had this shipped, two sectors would have under-counted permanently while the
            ranked list continued to look entirely reasonable. This is the argument for
            validating a mapping against its source scheme on every build rather than
            writing it once and trusting it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </>
    ) : (
      <p>The sector mapping has not been built yet.</p>
    )
  }

  <h2>Scoring model</h2>

  <p>
    The score is intended to be transparent and decomposable: no black box, weights
    visible and adjustable in the interface, and every rank breaking down into the
    components that produced it so a reviewer can disagree with a weighting and
    immediately see what changes.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Signal</th>
          <th scope="col">Rationale</th>
          <th scope="col">Note</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Filing volume, trailing 3y</td>
          <td>Baseline IP activity</td>
          <td>Normalize by company size where possible</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Filing velocity, YoY change</td>
          <td>Growth signals new legal need</td>
          <td>Expected to be the strongest single signal</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Technology concentration</td>
          <td>Focused portfolios suggest strategic IP programs</td>
          <td>HHI across CPC classes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Portfolio recency</td>
          <td>Recent activity beats historical volume</td>
          <td>Weight recent years more heavily</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="callout callout-limit">
    <span class="callout-label">Not yet built</span>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0">
      The scoring model runs on the USPTO patent tables, which have not been acquired
      yet. No scores, ranks, or company names appear on this page until they are
      computed from real data.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What this model cannot tell you</h2>

  <p>
    Stating this next to the results is the point, not a hedge. Every objection below is
    one an interviewer would raise, and each is a genuine weakness of the approach.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>It says nothing about existing counsel.</strong> A company with intense
      patent activity may already be well served by a firm it has used for a decade. The
      score measures demand signals, not availability, and cannot distinguish a prospect
      from someone else's long-standing client.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It lags reality by the grant delay.</strong> The dataset covers granted
      patents. The gap between filing and grant means the most recent years are
      systematically under-represented, so a company that started filing heavily last
      quarter is close to invisible here.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It over-weights cultural patenters.</strong> Some organizations patent
      prolifically for reasons of internal incentive or defensive posture rather than
      strategy. Volume-based signals rank them highly regardless of whether they
      represent real legal spend.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sector labels describe technology, not companies.</strong> A conglomerate
      appears in whichever sector it patents in, which may be nothing like how it
      describes itself. These are not company classifications.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Subclass-level mapping is coarse.</strong> Some irrelevant patents are
      swept in at the edges of each sector. The claim is that rankings are stable under
      that noise — a claim the scoring phase has to test rather than assume.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Data provenance</h2>

  <ProvenanceBlock
    record={patentProvenance}
    pendingLabel="USPTO patent tables — not yet acquired"
    pendingNote="The PatentsView API named in the original brief was retired in March 2026. The underlying tables survive as bulk downloads on the USPTO Open Data Portal, which requires a signed-in account. Until those files are in hand and inspected, this page shows no patent figures at all."
  />

  <ProvenanceBlock record={cpcProvenance} />
</Base>

<style>
  h4 {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    margin: 2.5rem 0 0.5rem;
  }

  .cpc-title {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    min-width: 16rem;
  }

  td:last-child {
    min-width: 18rem;
  }
</style>
